OpenChronology server for Debian and Ubuntu
===========================================

Install
-------

    sudo apt install ./openchronology-server_<version>_amd64.deb

apt installs the PostgreSQL server this package needs. (`dpkg -i` does not install
dependencies; after it, run `sudo apt -f install`.) The first installation:

  - creates the `openchronology` system user;
  - writes /etc/openchronology/openchronology.env, with a new AUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY;
  - creates the `openchronology` PostgreSQL role and database in the local cluster
    ("main"), with the bundled pgmp extension;
  - migrates the database (openchronology-migrate) and starts openchronology.service,
    which listens on 127.0.0.1:5173.

If PostgreSQL is not running at that point, start it and finish the setup with
`sudo dpkg-reconfigure openchronology-server`.

Configure
---------

Edit /etc/openchronology/openchronology.env, then `sudo systemctl restart openchronology`:

  - APP_ORIGIN: the public HTTPS origin users reach, without a trailing slash.
  - RESEND_API_KEY and AUTH_EMAIL_FROM for account email, then
    `sudo systemctl enable --now openchronology-mail`.
  - ADMIN_USERNAME, ADMIN_EMAIL and ADMIN_INITIAL_PASSWORD for the first administrator,
    created by `sudo openchronology-migrate`.
  - Sign-in providers, human verification, quotas: see the comments in the file and
    authentication.md, administration.md in this directory.

Back up AUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY apart from the database: without it, enrolled authenticator
secrets cannot be decrypted. Do not change it once accounts exist.

Serve it publicly through an HTTPS reverse proxy. With Caddy:

    timescale.example {
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:5173
    }

Caddy 2.5 and later replace an incoming X-Forwarded-For with the client address, so set
TRUST_PROXY=1 (with another proxy, only if it does the same; see README.md). Proxies
should allow request bodies of at least 32 MiB and streaming responses (live updates).

The syslog receiver (syslog.md) is configured in /etc/openchronology/syslog.env with routes
in /etc/openchronology/syslog-routes.json; then
`sudo systemctl enable --now openchronology-syslog`.

Upgrade
-------

Installing a newer package stops the server and leaves the database and configuration as
they are. It then lists:

  1. Settings this version's template has that /etc/openchronology/openchronology.env
     lacks. Compare with:
         diff -u /etc/openchronology/openchronology.env \
                 /usr/share/openchronology/openchronology.env.example
  2. A backup:  sudo -u postgres pg_dump openchronology > openchronology.sql
  3. sudo openchronology-migrate, which migrates the database and starts the server.

openchronology-migrate is safe to run more than once. `--no-restart` migrates only.

Remove
------

`sudo apt remove openchronology-server` stops and removes the server and keeps the
configuration and database. `sudo apt purge openchronology-server` also removes
/etc/openchronology. The database is never dropped automatically:

    sudo -u postgres dropdb openchronology
    sudo -u postgres dropuser openchronology

Layout
------

  /usr/lib/openchronology            the application, its Node.js runtime and och-convert
  /usr/sbin/openchronology-migrate   database migration
  /etc/openchronology                configuration (created on first install)
  /usr/share/openchronology          configuration templates of the installed version
  /usr/lib/systemd/system            openchronology, openchronology-mail, openchronology-syslog
  /usr/lib/postgresql/@PG_MAJOR@/lib, /usr/share/postgresql/@PG_MAJOR@/extension   pgmp
